WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.680
Since the Russian invasion began, Ukranians&nbsp;
have shared recipes for making Molotov cocktails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.000
and instructions for driving&nbsp;
abandoned troop carriers.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.360
They’ve used encrypted apps to coordinate&nbsp;
tactics and to ask Russians to stand up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.360
their government, who in turn have staged&nbsp;
protests in Moscow and other cities.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.080
Though it may end up losing in the battlefield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.400
Ukraine has been able to show the world the&nbsp;
brutality and folly of the Russian attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.240
which is only possible because everyday&nbsp;
citizens have maintained access to the internet.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.520
But maybe not for long: in areas with the heaviest&nbsp;
fighting, internet outages are becoming common,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.040
and since information is power in the&nbsp;
battlefield, there’s a danger that Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.360
will find a way to knock the country fully&nbsp;
offline.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.080
Which is why Ukraine’s Minister&nbsp;
of Digital Transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.560
and Vice Prime Minister tweeted a plea to&nbsp;
Elon Musk, “We ask you to provide Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.320
with Starlink stations and to&nbsp;
address sane Russians to stand.”

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.080
“Starlink service is now active in&nbsp;
Ukraine.” Musk tweeted back later that day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.800
collapsing a regulatory process that can take&nbsp;
months or years into under 280 characters.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.880
Starlink, up and running since&nbsp;
2021, is a global satellite internet provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.520
owned by Musk’s company SpaceX, which aims&nbsp;
to provide low latency, high speed internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.080
to areas that are less densely populated and&nbsp;
where fast reliable internet may be lacking.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.000
The first obstacle is that Ukranians can’t&nbsp;
just connect directly to Starlink satellites:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.920
First, they need ground terminals.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.720
“…terminals en route,” was how Musk finished&nbsp;
his tweet, and less than 48 hours later Fedorov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.840
replied with a picture showing a truckload of them&nbsp;
in Ukraine, “Starlink — here. Thanks, Elon Musk”

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.720
Those terminals will need to be brought into&nbsp;
cities under siege, and connected to wi-fi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.120
in turn allowing Ukranians&nbsp;
to connect their devices.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.560
And if terminals lose power, they’ll need&nbsp;
batteries or generators to stay online.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.280
But if the terminals can be&nbsp;
installed and maintained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.480
Starlink could provide a digital&nbsp;
lifeline to some Ukranians.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.840
Divorcing the online world from&nbsp;
geography and placing it outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.800
state control is in keeping with&nbsp;
the internet's original promise.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.720
The dream of the internet was one of complete&nbsp;
deterritorialization. Internet was supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:19.440
mean that it doesn't matter where you live.&nbsp;
You are connected to all of humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.720
through this completely transparent network&nbsp;
that where geography is irrelevant. We've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.760
how far short of that we have fallen. We've&nbsp;
seen some governments censor the internet

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.680
Eli Dourado is a senior research fellow&nbsp;
at the Center for Growth and Opportunity.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.616
I think a great outcome for the internet would&nbsp;
have been for the U.S. to be able to export the

00:02:38.616 --> 00:02:44.960
First Amendment to the entire planet, to be&nbsp;
able to say, well, if you're a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:51.840
that is based in the U.S. and you're serving the&nbsp;
entire planet and you're subject to U.S. law alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.596
then the First Amendment applies everywhere and&nbsp;
nobody can, can stop you from speaking freely.

00:02:57.596 --> 00:03:06.560
In reality it hasn't worked out that way. The internet is largely&nbsp;
tied to the politics and geography of earth.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.200
Can Starlink help make good on the internet’s&nbsp;
original promise to be a tool of liberation?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.320
Imagine if Putin had to answer to a populace&nbsp;
with immutable Internet access. Where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.800
could see the unvarnished reality of war. How&nbsp;
might that change the way a conflict plays out?

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.440
One of Starlink’s key innovations is that the&nbsp;
signal can be relayed laterally a number of times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.040
across country borders, time zones, even oceans,&nbsp;
before beaming back down to a ground station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.640
That could make it harder for any one country to&nbsp;
censor or track what its citizens access online.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.760
But only if Starlink first turns&nbsp;
on service in those places and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.560
routes internet traffic through ground&nbsp;
stations in neighboring countries..

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.280
Starlink is, uh, you know, of course, a&nbsp;
commercial offering from an American company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.080
that's launching frequently into space&nbsp;
under the outer space treaty, the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.160
States government is ultimately responsible&nbsp;
for what SpaceX does when it's in space.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.440
While the technology would allow for it, Starlink&nbsp;
may not turn out to serve that grand purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.560
So far, it’s only available in&nbsp;
countries that welcome its presence.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.040
But the technology holds promise .

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.000
Satellite internet might one day&nbsp;
offer an uncensored alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.440
for people living in hermit&nbsp;kingdoms, behind

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.400
great firewalls,&nbsp;&nbsp;or on information islands. 

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.360
And Everyday Ukranians are putting&nbsp;
truth to power. Coordinating their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.040
defense. Appealing directly to Russian kinship and&nbsp;
a common humanity. Rallying people to their cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:38.880
Showing the devastating cost of war. Starlink&nbsp;
could help them to prevail in that battle.

